    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into some data regarding rotor temperature spikes during high-speed deceleration tests. Could you explain the primary differences in how ceramic vs. semi-metallic brake pads handle heat dissipation and thermal soak?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic brake pads are made of inorganic materials and typically exhibit excellent resistance to heat, quickly dissipating heat through their porous structure. In contrast, semi-metallic brake pads contain organic materials mixed with metallic fibers to enhance heat dissipation. During high-speed deceleration, ceramic pads can cause less thermal soak, remaining cooler than semi-metallic pads, which might retain more heat due to higher friction and potentially lead to longer cooling times. However, semi-metallic pads usually have better initial bite and longer lifespan.",
      "source": "target",
