    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One thing I'm seeing in some of our recent telemetry is that the semi-metallic pads seem to be \"acting up\" or behaving unpredictably under extreme thermal stress, almost like there's an intermittent component failure causing sudden, erratic temperature surges. What are the most common mechanical causes for that kind of erratic behavior in the braking assembly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Intermittent component failure under extreme thermal stress in semi-metallic brake pads, like unexpected temperature surges, can be attributed to several mechanical causes. The first one could be a deterioration of the bonding matrix holding the metallic fibers together, leading to uneven pad wear and inconsistent friction surfaces. Another cause might be contamination of the pad-washing solutions with metal shavings or debris from grinding out old brake components, reducing cleaning efficacy and leading to uneven heat distribution. Noise from a failing ABS (Anti-lock Braking System) can also induce high-frequency oscillations that contribute to unpredictable thermal behavior. Lastly, an improperly adjusted or failing caliper piston can cause uneven pad wear due to the uneven contact with the rotor surface. Investigating each of these aspects could uncover the underlying cause of the erratic behavior.",
      "source": "target",
