      "content": "Localized pitting on the rotor surface signals that a foreign object may have been wedged into the space between the brake rotor and pad. In braking assemblies, this foreign object could be a remnant of hardware, contamination from the brake pad process, or perhaps even metal debris from headphone jacks or wiring harnesses. The object's presence could cause a small, localized hotspot during braking due to its higher thermal conductivity or friction properties. Such irreversible damage is not just a wear problem; it's a sign of a mechanical obstruction. This finding warrants a meticulous inspection of the assembly to identify the foreign object and assess if there's an analogy with the debris seen in the pad-washing solutions. Recurring resolve for these anomalies is necessary to maintain safe and predictable braking performance and to prevent unexpected thermal breakdown.",
